A||||8||Dan86gt3.8||Do drop springs affect drivability?||||14||
Z||000000||Dan86gt3.8||04-11-2000||11:29 AM||||Ya I was wondering sinse the fiero almost already sits on the ground, I was worried about doing sport springs or"drop springs".  I love how it corners already,but do yall have problems with scraping?  I have a reall steep driveway and live in a hilly part of texas.  So I am really catious of doing drop springs, I guess I will jsut stick to better sway bars and poly.||||reg||14
Z||000001||theogre||04-11-2000||08:30 PM||||doesn't matter what car..... yes they affect driving.<P>Fiero actually has the same ground clearance as most cars much larger. Minimum ground clearance is federally regulated.<P>Check your RPO codes against the list for your year. There are multiple spring options available stock. (Many RPO codes are the same for all years but not all.) the generic list is online I forget where. The year specific ones are in Alldata. A local repair shop may let you look it up.<P>there's the base set... can be mushy. poly and good shocks/struts are an absolute must.<P>There's a F41 package used in the sport coupes like mine. Very stiff.<P>Then there's WS6 that is F41 with tuned shocks and struts and a couple other things all of what can be added to F41 or any other "handling" option. WS6 on a Fiero only means so much now anyway..... the tuned shocks and struts are likely shot, and the bushings may need work on some or all. Anything not factory basically eliminates/alters the WS6 option from it's specialness.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||lowCG||04-11-2000||10:15 PM||||After meeting a guardrail that was also federally regulated in a car that had stiffer springs,but,was not lower by very much than a stock car,I'll say that everything crunched like it was supposed to,and my new car now hovers at a stance only 1/2" from stock height as well.<BR>Ride heights are setup for certain safety reasons,so when some moron hits you,you can safely spin into a closely matching barrier.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||04-12-2000||02:28 AM||||would a 1" drop affect the handling enough to notice a big difference?||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||04-12-2000||06:34 PM||||Drop springs do change your front suspension geometry a little. With only 1" lower springs, you'd probably not notice much difference in "normal" street driving. It would be most noticeable during high performance driving. I have a 2" drop on my Batmobile and am putting on a big front air dam. I probably will avoid speed bumps, big dips, and steep driveways if I want to keep that air dam intact.||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||04-12-2000||09:03 PM||||If you guys are looking for better handling from your fiero's but don't want to scrape the pavement on every entryway in the city and have a harsher ride than you already have than don't lower your fiero. Instead go with a modification that has the single biggest affect over any other suspension modification you can do to your fiero. Get really good tires or go with bigger quality tires.  The tires are the car’s connection between the car and the road, they can make a car ride hard or soft and handle responsive or vague. Add a sway bar to the back and some new bushings and shocks and you will have a good riding fiero that can handle the corners as well as those speed bumps and onramps. The fiero is already a nimble car anyway, what the heck do you guys want, a Formula-1 racecar or something? A fiero with a good stock suspension and just a tire size upgrade will pull over .90 gs on the skid pad.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||04-12-2000||10:44 PM||||88Formula is right, but you know us hard-core car-guys, we always have to "improve" things on our cars. I'm going all out on my Batmobile, but it's a toy. However, my daily driver Fiero stays with stock suspension for all of the reasons that 88Formula mentioned.||||reg||1
Z||000007||BroCar 87||04-12-2000||10:58 PM||||hey  theogre,  Where would someone find F41 suspension (WS6)!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Standard||04-13-2000||12:51 AM||||The WS6 was an option on the SE and came standard on the GT and Formula.  I don't think you could get it on the coupe, and I'm not sure about the sport coupe.  Like Ogre said up there, it doesn't really mean too much nowadays, other than for bragging rights.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  ||||reg||1
